---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import CommandOutput from '../../components/CommandOutput.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI applications" activeNav="/cli/applications">
  <h1>Applications</h1>
  <p>
    An application is the container everything else hangs off: identifiers, credentials,
    builds, files, and its icon and splash screen. Apps are addressed by slug, and the slug
    appears in every app-scoped URL.
  </p>

  <h2><code>appmctl apps</code></h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl apps list
appmctl apps get SLUG
appmctl apps create --name NAME [--slug SLUG] [--description DESC] [--metadata JSON] [--blueprint-id UUID]
appmctl apps update SLUG [--name NAME] [--description DESC] [--metadata JSON] [--blueprint-id UUID|--no-blueprint]
appmctl apps delete SLUG`} />

  <h3>List</h3>
  <CommandOutput command="appmctl apps list" capture="apps-list" />

  <h3>Show one app</h3>
  <CommandOutput command="appmctl apps get customer-app" capture="apps-get" />
  <p>
    Add <code>--json</code> to see everything the API returns, including metadata and the
    asset URLs:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --json apps get customer-app | jq '.data | {slug, name, metadata}'`} />

  <h3>Create, update, delete</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--slug</code> is optional on create; the API derives one from the name when it is
    omitted. <code>update</code> requires at least one field, and rejects a call that would
    change nothing.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl apps create --name "Customer App" --slug customer-app \\
  --description "Consumer-facing mobile client." \\
  --metadata '{"owner":"mobile-team"}'

appmctl apps update customer-app --description "Consumer client, iOS and Android."

# Attach or detach an optional blueprint.
appmctl apps update customer-app --blueprint-id "$BLUEPRINT_ID"
appmctl apps update customer-app --no-blueprint

appmctl apps delete customer-app`} />
  <p>
    Deleting an app removes its identifiers, credentials links, builds, files, and assets.
    Preview the request first if you are unsure which app a slug refers to:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --dry-run apps delete customer-app

# dry-run: no request sent
# method: DELETE
# url: https://manager.example.com/api/v1/acme-mobile/apps/customer-app`} />

  <h2><code>appmctl assets</code></h2>
  <p>
    Assets are the app icon and the splash screen. An app holds at most one of each, so
    <code>upload</code> replaces whatever is there. Beyond the original file, the platform
    generates per-platform variants — iOS icon sizes, Android density buckets, splash
    resolutions — and each variant is downloadable on its own.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl assets list [--app APP]
appmctl assets show [--app APP] --type icon|splashscreen [-o FILE]
appmctl assets download [--app APP] --type icon|splashscreen [--variant VARIANT] [-o FILE]
appmctl assets upload [--app APP] --type icon|splashscreen --file FILE
appmctl assets delete [--app APP] --type icon|splashscreen`} />

  <h3>Discover what exists</h3>
  <p>
    <code>list</code> prints the download URL for each original plus every variant that has
    actually been generated. A variant missing from this list has not been produced yet, and
    downloading it answers <code>404</code>:
  </p>
  <CommandOutput command="appmctl assets list" capture="assets-list" />

  <h3>Download</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--variant</code> defaults to <code>original</code>. Use the variant name exactly as
    <code>list</code> prints it, without the asset prefix — <code>icon.ios-icon-40</code> in
    the list is <code>--type icon --variant ios-icon-40</code> here:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl assets download --type icon -o icon.png
appmctl assets download --type icon --variant ios-icon-40 -o icon-40.png
appmctl assets download --type splashscreen --variant android-splash-port-xhdpi -o splash.png`} />
  <p>
    <code>show</code> streams the original inline rather than as an attachment, which is
    handy for a quick preview.
  </p>

  <h3>Upload and delete</h3>
  <p>
    Uploads must be images and are capped at 10 MB. Replacing an icon triggers an Expo sync
    when the app is linked to an Expo project:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl assets upload --type icon --file ./icon-1024.png
appmctl assets upload --type splashscreen --file ./splash.png

appmctl assets delete --type splashscreen`} />
  <p>
    Variants are regenerated after an upload, so <code>list</code> can briefly show fewer
    entries than it will a moment later.
  </p>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>apps:read</code> and <code>assets:read</code> for the read commands;
    <code>apps:write</code> and <code>assets:write</code> for the writes. Asset writes also
    need the <code>manage_applications</code> team permission. Attaching a known blueprint uses
    <code>apps:write</code>; managing the blueprint itself uses the separate scopes documented under
    <a href="/cli/blueprints">Blueprints</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
